Ditto.

A dive into Joseph Campbell's tome on mythology
should produce _lots_ of demigods, like
Hercules/Heracles, or Orpheus, who had to undergo
quests. A nice blend of a lot of the nastier quests
from various mythoi could be turned into a zinger of
a followup game with many alternatives for many
classes and races to pursue.

The good side of modeling quests after existing ones
is that you don't have to dumb the demigod back down
to some putz to start, you just upgrade the enemy,
instead.

FWIW

xanthian.

Imagine _starting_ with titans, liches, and dragons
as low level monsters instead of kobolds, goblins,
and newts, and a pet baby minotaur or balrog pup or
such at your side. A sequel to NetHack should be a
game where you rarely reach the second level alive,
with mere NetHack experience to guide your play, so
that the game learning curve is like NetHack's
again.

Game levels should be whole countrysides or Mansions
of E, instead of a few small rooms.

Entity ID's should be 16 bit instead of 8, to allow
for many more monster types, object types, terraine
types.

Multiple quests should be chained, with alternative
paths among the chains, so that you need, say, to
do the equivalent of beheading the hydra a mere 255
times before its head regeneration limit is reached,
to grab from its stash a cloak of skulking with
which to sneak into Hades to rescue Euridice who
alone knows just where the entry to the quest for
the ring of the Niebelung sits, ... but some
completely different set of quests might find
another entrance, ...

Which brings to mind that the adventurer should
_start_ the game with a random NetHack-style
artifact or two, one weapon, one not...



